  "title": "The dominant 7th chord",
  "summary": "A major triad plus a minor 7th: the chord that pulls home.",
  "minutes": 6,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "Dominant 7th chord",
      "body": "A major triad with a minor 7th on top (10 half steps above the root) is a **dominant 7th chord**, written with just a 7: G7 is G B D F. It is the V chord of a key with its 7th added, so in C major it is G7.\n\n**Why it matters:** It holds a tritone (B and F) that pulls hard to the tonic chord. V7 to I is the strongest \"we're home\" move there is, and the blues uses 7 chords everywhere.",
      "exampleMidi": [
        55,
        59,
        62,
        65
      ],
      "labels": {
        "55": "G (root)",
        "59": "B (3rd)",
        "62": "D (5th)",
        "65": "F (7th)"
      }
    },
    {
